A||||8||stsmithgt||something's howling at me!||||9||
Z||000000||stsmithgt||09-11-2000||09:47 AM||||Unfortunatly I picked up some sort of oscilating howl best heard at about 63 mph.+ or -.  Coinsidentaly(spelling?) I had spun out around a curve and slid into some dirt a couple days earlier.  It's the first time I lost it around a curve. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I don't usually drive like that, but this dog leg curve snuck up on me while I was going about 60 mph. in an alomond orchard at about 3:00 a.m..  It was unmarked and dark out and looked like it should have been taken at about 15 mph.   I'm just glad I didn't slide up into the orchard and wrap around one of the thousands of trees that lined the road.  I kept it in the dogleg long enough into the second curve to slow down and only slide into the dirt up to the barb wire fence.  MY question IS.... <BR>Is there something common that may tweak out of shape from spinnning a car around backwards or sliding it sideways into some dirt?  Bearing? gear? shaft? CVJ? Axle?  I didn't think it was a rough ride, but that howl is coming from something.  Below 45 mph. driving, you'd never know it was there.  Sorry about making this so long, but if you made it this far I hope you have some input. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thanks||||reg||9
Z||000001||stsmithgt||09-11-2000||09:53 AM||||Oops. oh yeah, I have an 86 GT with a manual 4 spd. duh. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-11-2000||10:34 AM||||  I noticed after I took the rear compartment insulation (best GM design for promoting rust & fires) out from around the battery tray and shock towers and noticed that my car would "howl" or have a harmonic resonance boom at about 60 mph too.  It could just be the nature of the spaceframe.  I'm pretty sure my halfshafts and bearings are good.  Mine's an 84 2.5L 4-spd  Anyone else notice this?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phoenix||09-11-2000||12:19 PM||||I also have a howl in my 88 that goes away in a right turn.  I think I have isolated it to the front left wheel bearing.  Going straight  it is hard to tell where the howl comes from as the whole car seems to have a harmonic resonance to it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||09-11-2000||12:39 PM||||Yeah.  Most of those noises I heard are ok, but this last noise just appeared after my encounter with that wicked dogleg mishap.  My car has been down a dirt road once before a year ago and that was going forward.  this sideways action I put it through I think shook something loose or made a flat spot on a bearing?  I don't know.  I just know it's a new sound and I don't like it.  I think I'll put it in the shop tomorrow to get another opinion, but I hate throwing money at it like that. I bought two sail panels for it last week.  I would of put that money towards more productive means of restoration mechanically than appearance.  Oh well mo money mo money my Fieros my honey. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||09-12-2000||09:38 AM||||OK.  I have a feeling it's an axle bearing.  I'm not sure of its configuration to the swing(A) arm, but I'm just gettting that feeling.  I think I can feel the vibration from the damage in the floor board.  If these roads in Monterey didn't suck so much I'd be more confident in my analysis.  You'd think in a place so populated with tourists, they'd keep the road beutiful for them to enjoy.  Damn, you need a 4WD on some of the roads around here.  I think that's why I'm looking around for a truck. Just in case I loose an axle or wheel and really screw things up.  <BR>Anyway thanks for your comments. I didn't take my car in to the shop yet.  I forgot I have a dental appointment today. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000006||Phoenix||09-17-2000||04:25 PM||||Update - Put in new left side wheel bearings this weekend, no more howl  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The front one was easy to tell that it was bad, turning by hand you could feel and hear the noise. The rear one didn't make any noise turning by hand but you could feel that it was bad.  Don't have to turn the radio up any more! YEEHAA||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||09-18-2000||10:17 AM||||I had the sound in the rear of my car identified as a bad hub.  I must of jammed the bearing when I slid it sideways through the dirt.  I'm waiting on parts now.  I hate driving it while it's bad, but it's my only mode of transportation.  The mechanic was messing with the rear suspension and got rid of the right handed pull I had.  I imagine it will be even better when I get the new hub put in.  The noise is getting louder and changing to more of a buzzing sound. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I hope I can turn my radio up loud enough.  It pushes my buttons to have somthing wrong with my car and have to wait for parts to come in.  I sure you all have had to wait for parts. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000008||stsmithgt||09-19-2000||09:54 AM||||Didn't make it through one day.  I smelled my transmission starting to cook and make noise.  Especially in second gear. Hmmmm?  I'm not stupid and am mechanicaly encline and I wish these people would realize that.  It sounds like he left my tranny empty!  My suspisions are someone took it out for a slamin' gear joy ride(test drive?) from what the tranny sounds like.  Second gear sounds trashed.  I don't do that sort of thing to this car.  I've done it to other vehicles I've owned, but not this one.  Needless to say I'm venting here.  Sorry for filling your ears with a bunch of boo hoo, but I hope you all have better luck than I am having. :l||||reg||9
